.BS
.SH NAME
tlschan \- TLS 1\&.2 and 1\&.3 channels on wolfSSL, rustls, Mbed TLS or OpenSSL
.SH SYNOPSIS
package require \fBTcl 9\&.0\fR
.sp
package require \fBtlschan ?0\&.1?\fR
.sp
package require \fBtlschan::wolfssl ?0\&.1?\fR
.sp
package require \fBtlschan::rustls ?0\&.1?\fR
.sp
package require \fBtlschan::mbedtls ?0\&.1?\fR
.sp
package require \fBtlschan::openssl ?0\&.1?\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::socket\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR? \fIhost\fR \fIport\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::socket\fR \fB-server\fR \fIcommand\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR? \fIport\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::import\fR \fIchannel\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBtlschan::starttls\fR \fIchannel\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBtlschan::unimport\fR \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::unstack\fR \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::handshake\fR \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::status\fR ?\fB-local\fR? \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::connection\fR \fIchannel\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::init\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.sp
\fBtlschan::ciphers\fR ?\fIprotocol\fR? ?\fIverbose\fR? ?\fIsupported\fR?
.sp
\fBtlschan::protocols\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::version\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::exporter\fR \fIchannel\fR \fIlabel\fR \fIlength\fR ?\fIcontext\fR?
.sp
\fBtlschan::randombytes\fR \fIcount\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::aesgcm-encrypt\fR \fIkey\fR \fIplaintext\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::aesgcm-decrypt\fR \fIkey\fR \fIdata\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::pbkdf2\fR \fIdigest\fR \fIpassword\fR \fIsalt\fR \fIiterations\fR \fIlength\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::pkgconfig\fR \fBlist\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::pkgconfig\fR \fBget\fR \fIkey\fR
.sp
\fBtlschan::backend\fR
.sp
.BE
.SH DESCRIPTION
These packages add TLS to Tcl channels by stacking a TLS layer on a socket
or any other bidirectional channel\&. Four backend packages share one channel
layer and one command set and differ only in the TLS library underneath:
.TP
\fBtlschan::wolfssl\fR
wolfSSL\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::rustls\fR
rustls with the ring crypto provider\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::mbedtls\fR
Mbed TLS 3\&.6\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::openssl\fR
OpenSSL 3\&.
.PP
Normally a program loads \fBtlschan\fR, which loads one backend and
makes its commands \fB::tlschan::\fR\fIname\fR\&. This page writes the
commands that way\&. Each backend package also defines them in the namespace
of its own name, such as \fB::tlschan::wolfssl\fR, with the same
syntax; requiring a backend directly pins it\&. Several backends can be loaded
into one interpreter, together with tcltls (package \fBtls\fR)\&.
.PP
\fBtlschan\fR takes the backends in the order given by the variable
\fB::tlschan::prefer\fR, set before loading it, else by the environment
variable \fBTLSCHAN_BACKEND\fR, else \fBopenssl rustls mbedtls wolfssl\fR\&.
The first of them already loaded is chosen, else the first that loads\&. If
none does, \fBpackage require tlschan\fR raises
\fBno TLS backend available (tried openssl, rustls, mbedtls, wolfssl)\fR
with errorCode \fBTLS BACKEND\fR\&.
.CS


namespace eval tlschan {variable prefer {mbedtls openssl}}
package require tlschan

.CE
.PP
Unless the command \fB::tls::socket\fR exists already, \fBtlschan\fR
also creates aliases \fB::tls::\fR\fIname\fR for the chosen backend's
commands, so that code written for tcltls runs unchanged\&. A tcltls loaded
afterwards replaces them\&. A backend package by itself never touches
\fB::tls\fR\&. See \fBCOMPATIBILITY\fR for differences from tcltls
and tclmtls\&.
.PP
Only TLS 1\&.2 and TLS 1\&.3 are implemented\&. By default a client opened with
\fBtlschan::socket\fR verifies the server's certificate chain against the
system trust store and checks that the certificate matches the host name it
connected to\&.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
\fBtlschan::socket\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR? \fIhost\fR \fIport\fR
Opens a TCP connection with \fB::socket\fR and stacks TLS on it with
\fBtlschan::import\fR\&. Returns the channel name\&. \fB-async\fR (which
takes no value), \fB-myaddr\fR and \fB-myport\fR go to \fB::socket\fR\&.
\fB-autoservername\fR \fIbool\fR, default \fB1\fR, sets
\fB-servername\fR to \fIhost\fR when it is not given\&. All other options,
after the defaults set with \fBtlschan::init\fR, go to
\fBtlschan::import\fR; see \fBOPTIONS\fR\&.
.sp
Errors from \fB::socket\fR (for example \fBcouldn't open socket: connection refused\fR)
and from \fBtlschan::import\fR are raised unchanged; when the import
fails the socket is closed\&. The handshake is not part of this command: it
runs on first use of the channel, see \fBCHANNEL BEHAVIOUR\fR\&. With
\fB-async\fR, a failed connect is reported there, as in
\fBhandshake failed: connection refused\fR\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::socket\fR \fB-server\fR \fIcommand\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR? \fIport\fR
Opens a listening socket with \fB::socket -server\fR and returns it\&.
\fB-myaddr\fR, \fB-reuseaddr\fR and \fB-reuseport\fR are passed to
\fB::socket\fR; \fB-backlog\fR is not supported\&. Every accepted
connection is imported with \fB-server\fR \fB1\fR,
\fB-model\fR set to the listening socket, and the other options, then
\fIcommand\fR is called with the channel, the client address and the client
port appended, as for \fB::socket -server\fR\&.
.sp
So all accepted connections share one configuration, and with it one
session ticket key (see \fBSESSION RESUMPTION\fR): the first
connection reads the files and evaluates the \fB-password\fR script, and
later ones reuse the result\&. An import error (an unreadable
\fB-certfile\fR, for example) closes that connection, is reported
through \fBbgerror\fR, and \fIcommand\fR is not called; the next connection
tries again\&. A \fB-model\fR given here replaces the listening socket\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::import\fR \fIchannel\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR?
.TP
\fBtlschan::starttls\fR \fIchannel\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR?
Stacks a TLS layer on \fIchannel\fR and returns the channel name, which is
unchanged\&. \fIchannel\fR can be any channel open for reading and writing\&.
No data is exchanged until the handshake starts, see
\fBCHANNEL BEHAVIOUR\fR\&. The options are described in
\fBOPTIONS\fR\&. \fBstarttls\fR is another name for \fBimport\fR\&.
.sp
Options are checked and files loaded here: a bad option, file or password
raises an error from this command and leaves the channel as it was\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::unimport\fR \fIchannel\fR
.TP
\fBtlschan::unstack\fR \fIchannel\fR
Sends a close_notify alert if the handshake has completed, writes out
pending TLS data and removes the TLS layer\&. The channel stays open and
carries plain data from then on\&. Raises
\fBbad channel "chan": not a TLS channel\fR (errorCode
\fBTLS CHANNEL\fR) if the topmost layer of \fIchannel\fR is not TLS\&.
\fBunstack\fR is another name for \fBunimport\fR\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::handshake\fR \fIchannel\fR
Advances the handshake\&. Returns \fB1\fR once the handshake has completed
and \fB0\fR if it is still in progress, which happens in nonblocking
mode and inside the channel's \fB-validatecommand\fR; otherwise the
command waits for the handshake to finish\&.
If the handshake failed, raises its error message, such as
\fBhandshake failed: certificate verify failed: certificate has expired\fR,
with errorCode \fBTLS HANDSHAKE\fR\&. A
failed channel raises the same error on every later call\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::status\fR ?\fB-local\fR? \fIchannel\fR
Returns a dictionary describing the session and the peer's certificate, or
with \fB-local\fR the local certificate\&. The dictionary is empty until
the handshake has completed, and stays empty if it failed\&. The certificate
keys are missing when there is no such certificate, for example on a server
whose client sent none\&. Session keys:
.RS
.TP
\fBprotocol\fR \fIstring\fR
\fBTLSv1\&.2\fR or \fBTLSv1\&.3\fR, as in tcltls\&.
.TP
\fBcipher\fR \fIname\fR
IANA name of the cipher suite, such as \fBTLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384\fR\&.
.TP
\fBsbits\fR \fIn\fR
Key size of the symmetric cipher in bits: \fB128\fR or \fB256\fR\&.
.TP
\fBalpn\fR \fIprotocol\fR
The protocol agreed through ALPN, or an empty string\&.
.TP
\fBservername\fR \fIname\fR
On a client the \fB-servername\fR in use; on a server the name the
client sent in the SNI extension\&. Empty if there is none\&.
.RE
.IP
The certificate keys are the same as in the \fIcert\fR argument of the
\fB-validatecommand\fR callback:
.RS
.TP
\fBcertificate\fR \fIpem\fR
The certificate in PEM format\&.
.TP
\fBsha1_hash\fR \fIhex\fR
.TP
\fBsha256_hash\fR \fIhex\fR
SHA-1 and SHA-256 digest of the DER certificate, in upper case hex\&.
.TP
\fBsubject\fR \fIdn\fR
.TP
\fBissuer\fR \fIdn\fR
Distinguished names in RFC 2253 form, most significant part last, as in
\fBCN=localhost,O=Example,C=US\fR\&. Special characters are escaped with a
backslash; attributes of unknown type appear as \fIoid\fR\fB=#\fR\fIhex\fR\&.
.TP
\fBserialNumber\fR \fIhex\fR
.TP
\fBserial\fR \fIhex\fR
The serial number in upper case hex without leading zeros, preceded by
\fB-\fR if negative\&. \fBserial\fR is the tcltls 1\&.7 name\&.
.TP
\fBversion\fR \fIn\fR
Certificate version, normally \fB3\fR\&.
.TP
\fBsignatureAlgorithm\fR \fIname\fR
.TP
\fBsignature\fR \fIname\fR
Signature algorithm of the certificate (outer and inner field), such as
\fBsha256WithRSAEncryption\fR or \fBecdsa-with-SHA256\fR; unknown
algorithms appear as dotted OIDs\&.
.TP
\fBnotBefore\fR \fItime\fR
.TP
\fBnotAfter\fR \fItime\fR
Validity period as \fBOct 15 08:03:54 2026 GMT\fR; the day of the
month is padded to two characters with a space\&.
.TP
\fBself_signed\fR \fIbool\fR
Whether issuer and subject are the same name\&.
.TP
\fBpublicKeyAlgorithm\fR \fIname\fR
Such as \fBrsaEncryption\fR, \fBid-ecPublicKey\fR or \fBED25519\fR\&.
.TP
\fBbits\fR \fIn\fR
Size of the public key: the RSA modulus length, or the curve size for
elliptic curve keys (\fB256\fR for P-256)\&. Missing if unknown\&.
.TP
\fBextensions\fR \fIlist\fR
Names of the certificate's extensions in certificate order, dotted OIDs
for unknown ones\&.
.TP
\fBnum_extensions\fR \fIn\fR
Length of that list\&.
.TP
\fBsubjectAltName\fR \fIlist\fR
Subject alternative names, each as \fBDNS:\fR\fIname\fR,
\fBIP Address:\fR\fIaddress\fR, \fBemail:\fR\fIaddress\fR,
\fBURI:\fR\fIuri\fR, \fBDirName:\fR\fI/name\fR or
\fBRegistered ID:\fR\fIoid\fR\&. IPv6 addresses are written as eight
colon separated hex groups\&. Missing without the extension\&.
.TP
\fBsubjectKeyIdentifier\fR \fIhex\fR
.TP
\fBauthorityKeyIdentifier\fR \fIhex\fR
Key identifiers in upper case hex\&. Missing without the extension\&.
.TP
\fBocsp\fR \fIlist\fR
OCSP responder URLs from the authority information access extension\&.
Missing if there are none\&.
.TP
\fBca\fR \fIbool\fR
The CA flag of the basic constraints extension; \fB0\fR without it\&.
.RE
.TP
\fBtlschan::connection\fR \fIchannel\fR
Returns a dictionary with the state of the connection, available at any
time:
.RS
.TP
\fBstate\fR \fIstate\fR
\fBhandshake\fR, \fBopen\fR or \fBfailed\fR\&.
.TP
\fBis_server\fR \fIbool\fR
Whether the channel is the server side\&.
.TP
\fBservername\fR \fIname\fR
As in \fBstatus\fR; before the handshake has completed, the
\fB-servername\fR given\&. Absent when the state is \fBfailed\fR\&.
.TP
\fBerror\fR \fImessage\fR
Only when the state is \fBfailed\fR: the error message\&.
.TP
\fBprotocol\fR \fBcipher\fR \fBsbits\fR \fBalpn\fR
Only when the state is \fBopen\fR: as in \fBstatus\fR\&.
.TP
\fBsession_reused\fR \fIbool\fR
Only when the state is \fBopen\fR: whether the session was resumed, see
\fBSESSION RESUMPTION\fR\&.
.RE
.TP
\fBtlschan::init\fR ?\fIoption value \&.\&.\&.\fR?
Sets the default options of \fBtlschan::socket\fR, both forms\&. Each
call replaces the previous defaults; without arguments it removes them\&.
\fB-model\fR, \fB-server\fR and \fB-servername\fR are dropped\&. The
options are not checked until \fBsocket\fR uses them\&.
\fBtlschan::import\fR does not use these defaults\&. Returns an empty
string\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::ciphers\fR ?\fIprotocol\fR? ?\fIverbose\fR? ?\fIsupported\fR?
Returns the IANA names of the cipher suites the library offers for
certificate based key exchange\&. \fIprotocol\fR is one of \fBssl2\fR,
\fBssl3\fR, \fBtls1\fR, \fBtls1\&.1\fR, \fBtls1\&.2\fR or
\fBtls1\&.3\fR; the list is empty for the first four\&. Without
\fIprotocol\fR, suites of both TLS 1\&.2 and TLS 1\&.3 are returned\&.
\fIverbose\fR and \fIsupported\fR are accepted for compatibility and
ignored\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::protocols\fR
Returns the protocols that can be enabled: \fBtls1\&.2 tls1\&.3\fR\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::version\fR
Returns the name and version of the TLS library, such as
\fBwolfSSL 5\&.9\&.4\fR, \fBrustls 0\&.23\&.46\fR,
\fBMbed TLS 3\&.6\&.7\fR or \fBOpenSSL 3\&.6\&.5\fR\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::exporter\fR \fIchannel\fR \fIlabel\fR \fIlength\fR ?\fIcontext\fR?
Returns \fIlength\fR bytes of keying material exported from the session as
defined by RFC 5705 (TLS 1\&.2) and RFC 8446 section 7\&.5 (TLS 1\&.3)\&.
\fIlength\fR is 1 to 8160; \fIcontext\fR is a byte string\&. With TLS 1\&.2
leaving out \fIcontext\fR gives a different result from an empty one; with
TLS 1\&.3 the two are the same\&. Errors:
\fBthe TLS handshake has not completed\fR (errorCode
\fBTLS WRONGSTATE\fR), \fBlength must be between 1 and 8160\fR
(\fBTLS WRONGARGS\fR) and \fBkeying material export failed\fR
(\fBTLS EXPORT\fR)\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::randombytes\fR \fIcount\fR
Returns \fIcount\fR bytes, 0 to 1048576, from the library's random number
generator\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::aesgcm-encrypt\fR \fIkey\fR \fIplaintext\fR
Encrypts \fIplaintext\fR with AES-256-GCM, without associated data\&.
\fIkey\fR must be 32 bytes\&. Returns a fresh random 12 byte nonce, the
ciphertext and the 16 byte tag, concatenated: 28 bytes more than
\fIplaintext\fR\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::aesgcm-decrypt\fR \fIkey\fR \fIdata\fR
Reverses \fBaesgcm-encrypt\fR: \fIdata\fR is nonce, ciphertext and tag as
returned by it\&. Raises \fBciphertext too short\fR if \fIdata\fR has
fewer than 28 bytes and \fBAES-GCM authentication failed\fR if the tag
does not match\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::pbkdf2\fR \fIdigest\fR \fIpassword\fR \fIsalt\fR \fIiterations\fR \fIlength\fR
Derives \fIlength\fR bytes, 1 to 64, from \fIpassword\fR and \fIsalt\fR
with PBKDF2 (RFC 8018)\&. \fIdigest\fR is \fBsha1\fR, \fBsha256\fR or
\fBsha512\fR, the HMAC hash\&. \fIiterations\fR is 1 to 10000000\&.
.sp
The arguments and results of \fBrandombytes\fR, \fBaesgcm-encrypt\fR,
\fBaesgcm-decrypt\fR and \fBpbkdf2\fR are byte strings; a character above
U+00FF raises a Tcl error, so convert text with
\fBencoding convertto utf-8\fR first\&. Their own errors have errorCode
\fBTLS CRYPTO\fR\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::pkgconfig\fR \fBlist\fR
.TP
\fBtlschan::pkgconfig\fR \fBget\fR \fIkey\fR
Build information as registered with \fBTcl_RegisterConfig\fR\&. Keys:
\fBversion\fR, the package version (\fB0\&.1\&.0\fR), and \fBbackend\fR,
one of \fBwolfssl\fR, \fBrustls\fR, \fBmbedtls\fR or
\fBopenssl\fR\&.
.TP
\fBtlschan::backend\fR
Only in \fBtlschan\fR: returns the name of the backend it loaded, such
as \fBopenssl\fR\&.
.PP
.SH OPTIONS
These options are accepted by \fBimport\fR and \fBstarttls\fR, by
\fBsocket\fR and by \fBinit\fR\&. Boolean values are any form Tcl accepts\&. An
empty value counts as not given for \fB-cadir\fR, \fB-cafile\fR,
\fB-cert\fR, \fB-certfile\fR, \fB-cipher\fR,
\fB-ciphersuites\fR, \fB-command\fR, \fB-key\fR,
\fB-keyfile\fR, \fB-model\fR, \fB-password\fR,
\fB-servername\fR, \fB-validatecommand\fR and \fB-vcmd\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-alpn\fR \fIlist\fR
Protocol names to negotiate with ALPN (RFC 7301), such as
\fBh2 http/1\&.1\fR\&. Each name must be 1 to 255 bytes long, or
\fBimport\fR raises \fBinvalid ALPN protocol name ""\fR (errorCode
\fBTLS CONFIG\fR)\&. Which common name is chosen depends on the backend,
see \fBBACKEND DIFFERENCES\fR\&. With none in common the handshake
completes without ALPN\&.
.TP
\fB-autoservername\fR \fIbool\fR
Only meaningful to \fBsocket\fR, see there\&. \fBimport\fR ignores it\&.
.TP
\fB-cadir\fR \fIdirectory\fR
A directory of trusted CA certificates\&. The TLS library reads it directly,
so it must be a native path, not one inside a Tcl virtual filesystem\&. The
expected file names differ, see \fBBACKEND DIFFERENCES\fR\&.
\fB-cadir\fR and \fB-cafile\fR can be combined\&.
.TP
\fB-cafile\fR \fIfile\fR
Trusted CA certificates: a PEM file with any number of them, or one DER
certificate\&. The file is read through Tcl's filesystem, so it can be inside
zipfs or another virtual filesystem\&.
.sp
Without \fB-cafile\fR and \fB-cadir\fR the system trust store is
used, read when first needed\&. Where it comes from depends on the backend,
see \fBBACKEND DIFFERENCES\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-cert\fR \fIdata\fR
The local certificate as data: PEM text, which may hold the leaf certificate
followed by intermediates to be sent along, or a single DER certificate\&.
Takes precedence over \fB-certfile\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-certfile\fR \fIfile\fR
As \fB-cert\fR, read from a file through Tcl's filesystem\&.
.TP
\fB-cipher\fR \fIlist\fR
The TLS 1\&.2 cipher suites to enable, replacing the default ones\&. The list
holds suite names separated by colons, commas or white space: IANA names
as \fBciphers\fR returns them
(\fBTLS_ECDHE_ECDSA_WITH_AES_128_GCM_SHA256\fR), the same with
\fB-\fR for \fB_\fR, OpenSSL names
(\fBECDHE-ECDSA-AES128-GCM-SHA256\fR) or the library's own names, in any
letter case\&. OpenSSL cipher string syntax (\fBHIGH\fR, \fB!aNULL\fR,
\fB@STRENGTH\fR) is not supported\&. An unknown name raises
\fBunknown cipher: \fR\fIname\fR (errorCode \fBTLS CONFIG\fR)\&.
.TP
\fB-ciphersuites\fR \fIlist\fR
The TLS 1\&.3 cipher suites to enable, replacing the default ones, in the
same form, such as \fBTLS_CHACHA20_POLY1305_SHA256\fR\&.
.sp
Names of either protocol may appear in either option: each name is sorted
to its protocol, and the names a protocol receives replace its defaults\&. A
protocol that receives no names keeps its defaults, unless its own option
was given: \fB-cipher\fR without a TLS 1\&.2 name disables TLS 1\&.2, and
\fB-ciphersuites\fR without a TLS 1\&.3 name disables TLS 1\&.3\&. If that
leaves no protocol, \fBimport\fR raises \fBno usable cipher suites\fR
(errorCode \fBTLS CONFIG\fR)\&.
.TP
\fB-command\fR \fIprefix\fR
A command prefix invoked on handshake completion and on failure, see
\fBCALLBACKS\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-key\fR \fIdata\fR
The private key as data, PEM or DER: PKCS#8, PKCS#1 (RSA) or SEC1 (EC)\&.
Takes precedence over \fB-keyfile\fR\&. A certificate and a key must be
given together, and they must match\&.
.TP
\fB-keyfile\fR \fIfile\fR
As \fB-key\fR, read from a file through Tcl's filesystem\&. Defaults to
the \fB-certfile\fR, so one PEM file can hold both\&.
.sp
An encrypted key needs \fB-password\fR\&. PKCS#8 keys encrypted with
PBES2, as written by \fBopenssl pkcs8 -topk8 -v2 aes-256-cbc\fR, work
with every backend, in PEM and DER form\&. Other formats depend on the
backend, see \fBBACKEND DIFFERENCES\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-model\fR \fIchannel\fR
Use a shared configuration: certificates, keys, password, trust,
protocols, cipher suites, ALPN, \fB-request\fR and \fB-require\fR
come from it, and options of this call that configure them are ignored\&.
\fB-servername\fR, \fB-command\fR and \fB-validatecommand\fR
still apply\&. \fIchannel\fR can be any channel\&. A TLS channel lends its own
configuration\&. Any other channel anchors one: the first import naming it
creates the configuration from its options, later imports share it, and it
is released when \fIchannel\fR is closed; channels using it keep it\&.
\fB-server\fR must match the configuration, or \fBimport\fR raises
\fB-model \fR\fIchannel\fR\fB is configured as a client\fR (or
\fBserver\fR), errorCode \fBTLS MODEL\fR\&. Sharing a configuration
saves loading files again and is needed for session resumption, see
\fBSESSION RESUMPTION\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-password\fR \fIscript\fR
A script whose result is the password of the private key; the script may
also end with \fBreturn\fR \fIpassword\fR\&. It is evaluated at global
level, once, by the \fBimport\fR that creates a configuration with a key,
whether or not the key is encrypted\&. An error in the script is raised by
\fBimport\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-request\fR \fIbool\fR
On a server, ask the client for a certificate\&. Default \fB0\fR\&. A
certificate the client sends is then available through \fBstatus\fR
whether or not it verifies, unless \fB-require\fR is set\&. No effect on a
client\&.
.TP
\fB-require\fR \fIbool\fR
On a client, verify the server's certificate chain and host name and fail
the handshake if they do not verify\&. Default \fB1\fR\&. With \fB0\fR, no
verification is done, unless \fB-validatecommand\fR is given\&.
.sp
On a server, require a client certificate that verifies against
\fB-cafile\fR, \fB-cadir\fR or the system store; implies
\fB-request\fR\&. Default \fB0\fR\&.
.TP
\fB-server\fR \fIbool\fR
Act as the server side\&. Default \fB0\fR\&. A server needs a certificate and
a private key, or \fBimport\fR raises an error\&.
.TP
\fB-servername\fR \fIname\fR
On a client, the name sent in the SNI extension and the name the server's
certificate must match\&. An IP address is matched against the certificate's
IP address entries and is not sent as SNI\&. Without \fB-servername\fR no
SNI is sent and only the chain is verified, not the name; unlike
\fBsocket\fR, \fBimport\fR does not set it\&. A value that is neither a host name
nor an IP address raises \fBinvalid server name "\fR\fIname\fR\fB"\fR
(errorCode \fBTLS CONFIG\fR)\&. Servers ignore this option\&.
.TP
\fB-ssl2\fR \fIbool\fR
.TP
\fB-ssl3\fR \fIbool\fR
.TP
\fB-tls1\fR \fIbool\fR
.TP
\fB-tls1\&.1\fR \fIbool\fR
.TP
\fB-castore\fR \fIuri\fR
.TP
\fB-dhparams\fR \fIfile\fR
.TP
\fB-post_handshake\fR \fIbool\fR
.TP
\fB-security_level\fR \fIn\fR
.TP
\fB-session_id\fR \fIdata\fR
Accepted for tcltls compatibility and ignored; the old protocols are never
enabled\&.
.TP
\fB-tls1\&.2\fR \fIbool\fR
.TP
\fB-tls1\&.3\fR \fIbool\fR
Enable TLS 1\&.2 or TLS 1\&.3\&. Both default to \fB1\fR\&. Disabling both
raises \fBno protocols enabled\fR (errorCode \fBTLS PROTOCOL\fR)\&.
.TP
\fB-validatecommand\fR \fIprefix\fR
.TP
\fB-vcmd\fR \fIprefix\fR
A command prefix that decides on each certificate the peer sends, see
\fBCALLBACKS\fR\&. A channel with this callback never resumes a session\&.
.PP
.SH CALLBACKS
\fB-command\fR and \fB-validatecommand\fR are command prefixes: the
arguments below are appended and the command is evaluated at global level\&.
They can run inside any command that does I/O on the channel, or from the
event loop when the channel is nonblocking\&. An error in a callback is
reported through \fBbgerror\fR (see \fBinterp bgerror\fR); it does not
become the result of the command that triggered the callback\&.
.TP
\fIprefix\fR \fBinfo\fR \fIchannel\fR \fBhandshake\fR \fBdone\fR \fB{SSL negotiation finished successfully}\fR \fBinfo\fR
\fB-command\fR, once, when the handshake has completed\&.
.TP
\fIprefix\fR \fBerror\fR \fIchannel\fR \fImessage\fR
\fB-command\fR, once, when the channel fails, during the handshake or
later\&. \fImessage\fR is the error that I/O on the channel raises from then
on\&.
.TP
\fIprefix\fR \fBverify\fR \fIchannel\fR \fIdepth\fR \fIcert\fR \fIok\fR \fIerror\fR
\fB-validatecommand\fR, during the handshake, for each certificate the
peer sent, from the highest \fIdepth\fR down to \fB0\fR, the peer's own
certificate\&. Certificates taken from the local trust store are not
reported\&. \fIcert\fR is a dictionary with the certificate keys of
\fBstatus\fR\&. \fIok\fR is \fB1\fR if the library accepts this
certificate and \fB0\fR if not; \fIerror\fR then gives the reason, such
as \fBcertificate has expired\fR, \fBhostname mismatch\fR or
\fBissuer certificate not trusted\fR for a certificate signed by a
rejected one, and is empty otherwise\&.
.sp
A result that is a true boolean or a nonzero integer accepts the
certificate\&. Anything else, or an error, rejects it, and the handshake
fails with \fBhandshake failed: certificate rejected by validation callback\fR\&.
The result replaces the library's verdict whatever \fB-require\fR says:
the callback can accept an expired or untrusted certificate or a name
mismatch, and can reject a valid one\&. A client calls it whenever it is
given; a server only with \fB-request\fR or \fB-require\fR, when
the client sends a certificate\&.
.sp
The callback may use its channel, but the handshake is still in progress:
\fBhandshake\fR returns \fB0\fR, reads return nothing, and what it
writes is sent once the handshake has completed\&.
.PP
The \fB-password\fR script is not a prefix: it is evaluated as given,
without arguments, see \fBOPTIONS\fR\&.
.SH "CHANNEL BEHAVIOUR"
.IP \(bu
The TLS channel keeps the name of the channel below\&. It adds no channel
options of its own: \fBchan configure\fR options other than the generic
ones, such as \fB-peername\fR, \fB-sockname\fR or
\fB-keepalive\fR, are those of the channel below\&.
.IP \(bu
The handshake starts on first use\&. On a blocking channel the first
\fBread\fR, \fBgets\fR, \fBflush\fR or \fBtlschan::handshake\fR performs the
whole handshake and waits for it\&. On a nonblocking channel the handshake
proceeds from the event loop: at once if the channel was already
nonblocking when imported, otherwise after the first read or write attempt
or once a \fBchan event\fR handler is set\&. \fBtlschan::handshake\fR also
advances it\&. Data written before it completes is buffered and sent
afterwards\&. Readable and writable handlers are not called before the
handshake has completed or failed\&.
.IP \(bu
A failure is final: every later \fBread\fR, \fBgets\fR, \fBputs\fR or
\fBflush\fR raises the error, without errorCode, and a nonblocking channel
becomes readable and writable to report it\&. During the handshake the
message is \fBhandshake failed: \fR\fIreason\fR; afterwards
\fBread failed: \fR\fIreason\fR, \fBwrite failed: \fR\fIreason\fR,
\fBconnection closed\fR or an operating system message such as
\fBconnection reset by peer\fR\&. Either way it can also be
\fBpeer sent alert: \fR\fIdescription\fR\&. Typical handshake reasons are
\fBcertificate verify failed: \fR\fIwhy\fR,
\fBcertificate rejected by validation callback\fR,
\fBpeer did not return a certificate\fR and
\fBconnection closed during handshake\fR; \fIwhy\fR is one of
\fBcertificate has expired\fR, \fBcertificate is not yet valid\fR,
\fBhostname mismatch\fR, \fBself-signed certificate\fR,
\fBunable to get local issuer certificate\fR or
\fBissuer certificate not trusted\fR, or else the library's own words\&.
.IP \(bu
In TLS 1\&.3 a client's handshake completes before the server has checked
the client certificate\&. A rejection reaches the client as an alert on its
next read, as in \fBpeer sent alert: certificate required\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
The end of the stream is reported as EOF both when the peer sent a
close_notify alert and when the connection was closed without one\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBclose\fR sends a close_notify alert if the handshake has completed,
writes out pending data, blocking if needed, and closes the channel below\&.
Tcl refuses to half-close a stacked channel: \fBclose\fR \fIchannel\fR
\fBwrite\fR raises
\fBhalf-close not applicable to stack of transformations\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBfcopy\fR and \fBchan copy\fR work in both directions\&.
.PP
.SH "SESSION RESUMPTION"
All backends resume sessions, in TLS 1\&.2 and TLS 1\&.3, by the same rules:
.IP \(bu
A server issues session tickets under a key that belongs to its
configuration, so it resumes only sessions of connections that shared that
configuration\&. The connections accepted by one \fBsocket -server\fR
share one\&.
.IP \(bu
A client keeps the most recent session for each server name, for up to 32
names, in its configuration, and offers it on the next connection to the
same \fB-servername\fR that uses this configuration\&. A client therefore
resumes only when its connections share a configuration through
\fB-model\fR, for example one anchored on a long-lived channel:
.CS


set model [file tempfile]
proc https {args} {
    tlschan::socket -model $::model {*}$args
}
http::register https 443 https

.CE
.IP \(bu
There is no resumption for a client without \fB-servername\fR, nor for
a client or a server with \fB-validatecommand\fR, whose callback must
see every handshake\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBconnection\fR reports whether a session was resumed as
\fBsession_reused\fR\&.
.PP
.SH "BACKEND DIFFERENCES"
The backends behave alike except for the following, observed with
wolfSSL 5\&.9\&.4, rustls 0\&.23\&.46, Mbed TLS 3\&.6\&.7 and OpenSSL 3\&.6\&.5\&.
.TP
Cipher suites
\fBtlschan::wolfssl\fR and \fBtlschan::rustls\fR have 3 TLS 1\&.3
suites and 6 TLS 1\&.2 suites, all ECDHE with AES-GCM or ChaCha20-Poly1305\&.
\fBtlschan::mbedtls\fR and \fBtlschan::openssl\fR add the CCM suites
to TLS 1\&.3 and offer many more TLS 1\&.2 suites (103 and 59 in the builds
above), including CBC, ARIA, Camellia, DHE and RSA key exchange; their lists
depend on how the library was built\&.
.TP
ALPN choice
\fBtlschan::wolfssl\fR chooses the first of the client's protocols that
the server also lists; the others choose the first of the server's protocols
that the client offers\&.
.TP
\fB-cadir\fR
\fBtlschan::wolfssl\fR and \fBtlschan::mbedtls\fR load every
certificate file in the directory\&. \fBtlschan::rustls\fR loads files
named "\fI*\&.pem\fR", "\fI*\&.crt\fR" or like an OpenSSL hash link
("\fI1a2b3c4d\&.0\fR")\&. \fBtlschan::openssl\fR needs an OpenSSL hashed
directory (see \fBopenssl rehash\fR) and finds only certificates under
their hash names\&.
.TP
Trust anchors
\fBtlschan::openssl\fR also accepts a certificate that is not a CA,
given in \fB-cafile\fR, as a trust anchor; the others do not\&.
.TP
System trust store
\fBtlschan::wolfssl\fR uses the Windows or macOS store, elsewhere the
certificates in "\fI/etc/ssl/certs\fR",
"\fI/etc/pki/ca-trust/source/anchors\fR" and "\fI/etc/pki/tls/certs\fR", or if
none exists the bundle "\fI/etc/ssl/cert\&.pem\fR" (on Haiku
"\fI/boot/system/data/ssl/CARootCertificates\&.pem\fR"); it ignores
\fBSSL_CERT_FILE\fR and \fBSSL_CERT_DIR\fR\&. \fBtlschan::rustls\fR uses
rustls-native-certs, which honours \fBSSL_CERT_FILE\fR and
\fBSSL_CERT_DIR\fR, and falls back to Mozilla's root list when the system
has none\&. \fBtlschan::mbedtls\fR reads \fBSSL_CERT_FILE\fR and
\fBSSL_CERT_DIR\fR if set, else the first CA bundle found at the usual
places, else "\fI/etc/ssl/certs\fR"; on Windows it uses the Windows
certificate store, on macOS the keychain's trust settings\&.
\fBtlschan::openssl\fR uses OpenSSL's default locations, which honour
\fBSSL_CERT_FILE\fR and \fBSSL_CERT_DIR\fR, then the usual bundle files, and
on Windows with OpenSSL 3\&.2 or later the Windows store\&.
.TP
Encrypted keys
Legacy encrypted PEM keys (\fBProc-Type: 4,ENCRYPTED\fR) and PKCS#8 keys
encrypted with PBES1, such as PBE-SHA1-3DES, work only with
\fBtlschan::mbedtls\fR and \fBtlschan::openssl\fR;
\fBtlschan::wolfssl\fR reports \fBunsupported key encryption\fR for
them\&.
.TP
Refused client certificates
The alert a server sends when it refuses a client certificate is the
library's\&. For a missing certificate it is \fBcertificate required\fR in
TLS 1\&.3; in TLS 1\&.2 \fBtlschan::wolfssl\fR and
\fBtlschan::openssl\fR send \fBhandshake failure\fR,
\fBtlschan::rustls\fR \fBcertificate required\fR, and
\fBtlschan::mbedtls\fR closes the connection without an alert, so the
client reports \fBconnection closed during handshake\fR\&. For an
untrusted certificate it is \fBunknown CA\fR, from
\fBtlschan::rustls\fR \fBcertificate unknown\fR\&. A
\fBtlschan::mbedtls\fR server in TLS 1\&.3 encrypts the alert with keys
the client no longer reads with, so the client's next read fails with a MAC
error instead (an Mbed TLS bug); the server still refuses\&.
.TP
Received alerts
\fBtlschan::mbedtls\fR reports a received alert as
\fBpeer sent alert\fR without its name: Mbed TLS 3\&.6 doesn't say which
one arrived\&.
.PP
.SH COMPATIBILITY
.SS TCLTLS
Code written for tcltls 2 mostly runs unchanged through the
\fB::tls\fR aliases of \fBtlschan\fR, or with \fB::tls\fR replaced
by \fB::tlschan\fR or a backend's namespace\&. The differences:
.IP \(bu
Not provided: \fBtls::shutdown\fR, \fBtls::misc\fR, \fBtls::build-info\fR,
the sample callbacks \fBtls::callback\fR, \fBtls::password\fR and
\fBtls::validate_command\fR, and \fBtls::debug\fR\&. Added:
\fBexporter\fR, \fBrandombytes\fR, \fBaesgcm-encrypt\fR,
\fBaesgcm-decrypt\fR and \fBpbkdf2\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBstatus\fR: \fBcipher\fR is an IANA name,
also for TLS 1\&.2, where tcltls uses OpenSSL names; digests are upper case
hex; \fBbits\fR is the key size, not its security strength\&. tcltls keys
not listed under \fBstatus\fR, such as \fBpeername\fR,
\fBverifyResult\fR, \fBkeyUsage\fR or \fBall\fR, are absent;
\fBself_signed\fR, \fBca\fR, \fBnum_extensions\fR and \fBocsp\fR
correspond to tcltls's \fBselfSigned\fR, \fBbasicConstraintsCA\fR,
\fBextCount\fR and \fBocspResponders\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBconnection\fR: \fBstate\fR is \fBhandshake\fR, \fBopen\fR or
\fBfailed\fR, not an OpenSSL state string, and only the keys listed
there exist\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBciphers\fR returns IANA names and ignores \fIverbose\fR and
\fIsupported\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
\fB-command\fR reports only \fBinfo\fR (handshake completion) and
\fBerror\fR; there are no \fBmessage\fR and \fBsession\fR calls\&.
.IP \(bu
\fB-validatecommand\fR is called only with \fBverify\fR, not with
\fBalpn\fR, \fBhello\fR or \fBsni\fR\&. Any true result accepts; tcltls
treats \fB2\fR as rejecting without aborting\&. Certificates from the local
trust store are not reported\&.
.IP \(bu
\fB-password\fR is evaluated without arguments; tcltls appends
\fBpassword\fR, the read/write flag and the maximum length\&.
.IP \(bu
\fB-cert\fR and \fB-key\fR take PEM as well as DER;
\fB-cafile\fR, \fB-certfile\fR and \fB-keyfile\fR are read through
Tcl's filesystem\&.
.IP \(bu
Options without an effect here are accepted and ignored, see
\fBOPTIONS\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
errorCodes begin with \fBTLS\fR, see \fBERRORS\fR\&.
.PP
.SS TCLMTLS
tclmtls is the package \fBmtls\fR with commands in \fB::mtls\fR;
its names do not clash with these packages\&. Compared with tclmtls:
.IP \(bu
There is nothing like \fBmtls::debug\fR or \fBmtls::build-info\fR\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBtlschan::pkgconfig\fR has only the keys \fBversion\fR and
\fBbackend\fR; tclmtls's \fBclient\fR, \fBserver\fR,
\fIproto\fR\fB,protocol\fR and \fBversion,backend\fR,
\fBname,backend\fR, \fBtype,backend\fR are absent\&.
.IP \(bu
\fBstatus\fR returns more keys, and cipher suites, there and in
\fBciphers\fR, are IANA names (\fBTLS_AES_256_GCM_SHA384\fR) instead of
Mbed TLS names (\fBTLS1-3-AES-256-GCM-SHA384\fR)\&.
.IP \(bu
errorCodes begin with \fBTLS\fR instead of \fBMTLS\fR, and errors of the
crypto commands are just \fBTLS CRYPTO\fR, without a further element\&.
The crypto commands take and return data in the same formats and with the
same limits\&.
.PP
.SH ERRORS
Errors raised by the commands have these errorCodes:
.TP
\fBTLS CONFIG\fR
An invalid option value: unparsable certificate, key or CA file, wrong
password, unknown cipher suite, invalid ALPN name or server name, missing
certificate on a server\&.
.TP
\fBTLS PROTOCOL\fR
No protocol enabled\&.
.TP
\fBTLS MODEL\fR
The \fB-model\fR configuration is for the other side, client or server\&.
.TP
\fBTLS IMPORT\fR
The session could not be set up\&.
.TP
\fBTLS CHANNEL\fR
The channel is not a TLS channel\&.
.TP
\fBTLS HANDSHAKE\fR
Raised by \fBhandshake\fR for a failed handshake\&.
.TP
\fBTLS WRONGSTATE\fR \fBTLS WRONGARGS\fR \fBTLS EXPORT\fR
Raised by \fBexporter\fR\&.
.TP
\fBTLS CRYPTO\fR
Raised by the crypto commands\&.
.TP
\fBTLS INIT\fR
The library could not be initialised when loading the package\&.
.TP
\fBTLS BACKEND\fR
\fBtlschan\fR found no backend to load\&.
.PP
Errors opening a file named in an option have a \fBPOSIX\fR errorCode;
errors in arguments have the usual \fBTCL\fR ones\&. I/O errors on the
channel do not set errorCode, see \fBCHANNEL BEHAVIOUR\fR\&.
.SH "STATIC LINKING"
Built with \fBBUILD_SHARED_LIBS=OFF\fR, each backend is an archive, such
as "\fIlibtcl9tlschan_wolfssl0\&.1\&.0\&.a\fR", whose only global symbol is its
initialisation function: \fBTlschan_wolfssl_Init\fR,
\fBTlschan_rustls_Init\fR, \fBTlschan_mbedtls_Init\fR or
\fBTlschan_openssl_Init\fR\&. The archive uses Tcl's stubs interface, so the
application also links Tcl's stub library; the pkg-config file
"\fItcl9tlschan_wolfssl\&.pc\fR" (and so on) names the other libraries to
link\&. The application registers the package and declares it in each
interpreter that is to use it:
.CS


Tcl_StaticLibrary(NULL, "Tlschan_wolfssl", Tlschan_wolfssl_Init, NULL);
Tcl_EvalEx(interp,
    "package ifneeded tlschan::wolfssl 0\&.1\&.0 {load {} Tlschan_wolfssl}", -1, 0);

.CE
Pass NULL, not an interpreter: with one, Tcl takes the package as already
loaded there and never calls \fBTlschan_wolfssl_Init\fR\&. There is no
safe-interpreter initialisation function\&.
.PP
\fBtlschan\fR is a script, installed with its "\fIpkgIndex\&.tcl\fR" in
"\fIlib/tlschan0\&.1\&.0\fR" for static builds too\&. An application ships it in
its script library, next to the backends it registers, and
\fBpackage require tlschan\fR then picks among those\&. See
"\fIREADME\&.md\fR" for building\&.
.SH EXAMPLES
Fetch a page over HTTPS, verifying the server against the system store:
.CS


package require tlschan
package require http
http::register https 443 ::tlschan::socket
set tok [http::geturl https://www\&.tcl-lang\&.org/]
puts [http::ncode $tok]
http::cleanup $tok

.CE
An echo server, event driven:
.CS


package require tlschan

proc accept {chan addr port} {
    chan configure $chan -blocking 0 -buffering line
    chan event $chan readable [list echo $chan]
}

proc echo {chan} {
    if {[catch {gets $chan line} n]} {
        puts stderr "$chan: $n"
        close $chan
    } elseif {$n >= 0} {
        puts $chan $line
    } elseif {[eof $chan]} {
        close $chan
    }
}

set opts {-certfile server\&.pem -keyfile server-key\&.pem}
tlschan::socket -server accept {*}$opts 8443
vwait forever

.CE
Switch an established plain connection to TLS, as after a STARTTLS
command; \fB-servername\fR must be given explicitly:
.CS


package require tlschan
set chan [socket mail\&.example\&.com 587]
# \&.\&.\&. plain text exchange ending in STARTTLS \&.\&.\&.
tlschan::starttls $chan -servername mail\&.example\&.com
tlschan::handshake $chan

.CE
Accept a server only if its certificate has a known SHA-256 fingerprint,
such as one shown by \fBopenssl x509 -noout -fingerprint -sha256\fR\&.
This one pins the backend by requiring it directly:
.CS


package require tlschan::mbedtls

proc pin {fingerprint op chan depth cert ok error} {
    if {$depth > 0} {
        return 1
    }
    set expected [string map {: ""} $fingerprint]
    string equal -nocase $expected [dict get $cert sha256_hash]
}

set vcmd [list pin $fingerprint]
set chan [tlschan::mbedtls::socket -validatecommand $vcmd device\&.local 8443]

.CE
Mutual TLS: the server requires a client certificate issued by its CA and
greets the client by name\&.
.CS


package require tlschan

proc accept {chan addr port} {
    chan configure $chan -blocking 0 -buffering line
    chan event $chan readable [list greet $chan]
}

proc greet {chan} {
    if {[catch {gets $chan line} n] || [eof $chan]} {
        close $chan
    } elseif {$n >= 0} {
        puts $chan "hello [dict get [tlschan::status $chan] subject]"
    }
}

set opts {-certfile server\&.pem -keyfile server-key\&.pem}
tlschan::socket -server accept -require 1 -cafile client-ca\&.pem {*}$opts 8443
vwait forever

.CE
The client:
.CS


package require tlschan
set opts {-certfile client\&.pem -keyfile client-key\&.pem}
set chan [tlschan::socket -cafile server-ca\&.pem {*}$opts server\&.example\&.com 8443]
chan configure $chan -buffering line
puts $chan hi
puts [gets $chan]

.CE
Code written for tcltls, unchanged:
.CS


package require tlschan
set chan [tls::socket www\&.tcl-lang\&.org 443]

.CE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
chan, http, socket
.SH KEYWORDS
ALPN, Mbed TLS, OpenSSL, SSL, TLS, X\&.509, certificate, channel, https, rustls, socket, wolfSSL
.SH CATEGORY
Networking
